Logging and annotation support:
- `logger=console` uses the standard console logger
- `logger=rich` uses the richer terminal logger
- `logger=file` requires `logger_file` and writes normal logs to that path
- `logger=none` disables normal logging output
- `logger=custom` uses the application-defined logger registered during initialisation
- `annotation=github` enables GitHub-compatible structured annotations
- `annotation=none` disables structured annotation output
- CLI defaults: `logger=console`, `annotation=none`

{{- if usesResult $fn }}

Result output and logging combinations:

| `output_format` | `output_file` | Allowed `logger` values | Behaviour | Allowed |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `human` | empty or `-` | `console`, `rich`, `file`, `none`, `custom` | The result is rendered through the configured logger on the normal logger stream. | yes |
| `human` | any file path | none | `output_file` is rejected because human output does not use the machine-readable file/output stream. | no |
| `json` or `yaml` | empty or `-` | `console`, `rich`, `file`, `none`, `custom` | The machine-readable result is written to standard output. `logger=console` and `logger=rich` are accepted but normal logging is routed away from stdout so the result stream stays clean. | yes |
| `json` or `yaml` | any file path | `console`, `rich`, `file`, `none`, `custom` | The machine-readable result is written to the selected file and normal logging follows the configured logger mode. | yes |

`logger=file` still requires `logger_file` in every allowed combination.

